{:edb/db-before-t 165 :edb/db-after-t 166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533478 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:44:12.199Z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399626 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399619 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399598 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084932 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399494 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186088038 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n$2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3\\cos x = 1 - 4y\\sin x$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186088038 1074 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102300 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399377 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1071 "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186106074 1071 "![](images/e2/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186106074 1074 "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399425 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399432 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115362 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399439 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186119769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399404 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186119769 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399411 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124508 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399507 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399549 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124593 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399556 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399528 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124594 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399535 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124603 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399605 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124603 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399612 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399480 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124655 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399521 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124656 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399514 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399542 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399563 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399570 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399577 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399584 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399591 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121487 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399446 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1071 "![](images/b6/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z = f(x, y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186124820 1071 "![](images/b6/b698fe735caaf3bc260c1e921bb95596df8869eba67ec9ce72484b4d86cc8c69.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124820 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence $A$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/0a/0a371aa559222bd28c37677bace18eb912bdc978e364bab4cb3d83f7a310de3f.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399383 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399390 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186133933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399397 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399452 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186172141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399459 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186176072 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186179782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399418 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399370 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399466 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399473 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399357 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1069 "q-161827" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1071 "![](images/9f/9f7ed51c1273b73243beb574535686f4ce3498b28c84cbe76833a3096674792c.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1072 #uuid "a0831acd-ddeb-5e04-811c-4d36ad3d1496" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/d3/d338181a6631c513330ef2dd3899cfb9b01cb6f4c4ff7d9cd65038b381e7fec4.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,3]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399361 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1080 #uuid "07b9fac4-36f4-5471-8875-46834f232265" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1081 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1084 #uuid "fdff4620-93ff-550e-afdc-c665cb0060db" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1081 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1084 #uuid "6fa29027-2d4f-5367-917f-52c169af3941" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1081 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{3},\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1084 #uuid "9a681f25-a3d3-54aa-8220-4047401f3201" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1081 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,3\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1084 #uuid "ebb3ebdb-8986-5120-bc0e-293da834f2da" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399362 1081 "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399362 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399362 1084 #uuid "ec03a2a0-b115-5ed7-a6b0-20a8edc98e96" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399364 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1069 "q-161879" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1071 "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z-3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1072 #uuid "4004275e-59e4-54d2-bbaa-29203ac29308" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399365 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399366 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399367 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399368 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399369 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399365 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1080 #uuid "6b627816-1293-53a9-a8e8-93bf2fb0fcef" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399365 1081 "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399365 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399365 1084 #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399366 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399366 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399366 1084 #uuid "2e6baa46-2362-5360-8c5f-7031d62e1411" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399367 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399367 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399367 1084 #uuid "7adad8b8-d3ef-52ef-b487-e4018ac1507b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399368 1081 "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399368 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399368 1084 #uuid "06bed278-63d1-5662-b5c3-13bc65e1ffc5" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399369 1081 "\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(uv)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-u" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399369 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399369 1084 #uuid "03678aa6-e1ae-5bec-8d2a-faf8e1de8ec2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399371 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399370 1069 "q-161902" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399370 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,y,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9$ and the plane $z=y-x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399370 1072 #uuid "f21c0998-8903-5ed2-945d-a8c8324dedb7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399370 1074 "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=y-x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=y,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-y\\cdot 1+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower cone ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9)=\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9)=\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=y,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y\\cdot \\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399372 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399373 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399374 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399375 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399376 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399375 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399371 1080 #uuid "531e02f0-754c-57e9-98aa-5097314bafb8" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399372 1081 "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399372 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399372 1084 #uuid "6df4d06f-b85b-5df3-8dc8-2bb8f495582f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399373 1081 "y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399373 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399373 1084 #uuid "1a198055-107c-5684-9d14-e7c623094522" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399374 1081 "1-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399374 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 1319413