_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4de9d9df51efcec67253a1c7118466f29459ffd5c9136c144d33500ec5557bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](images/18/18bb3e55f076e022176bb60df27d5665ed2d91d75d618969c04ce192378db922.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=4-2z.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-2z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2z}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399392 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399393 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399394 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399395 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399396 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399393 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399391 1080 #uuid "f079fa33-36ea-54da-8c9a-cfed3ba497cd" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399392 1081 "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399392 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399392 1084 #uuid "6f703bd0-2dd8-50a8-84be-59480e209b05" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399393 1081 "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399393 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399393 1084 #uuid "16f6fbe8-a535-5960-a6c7-b5dd576f6b56" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399394 1081 "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399394 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399394 1084 #uuid "6e9b1939-7256-56eb-8c7a-9b505da6122c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399395 1081 "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=3,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399395 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399395 1084 #uuid "e24c4541-f269-56ad-b95a-224e52a4e088" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399396 1081 "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399396 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399396 1084 #uuid "d0a313c4-3307-5cf2-9cf8-be47e0fcc064" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399398 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399397 1069 "q-162365" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399397 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399397 1072 #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399397 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399399 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399400 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399401 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399402 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399403 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399402 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399398 1080 #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399399 1081 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399399 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399399 1084 #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399400 1081 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399400 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399400 1084 #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399401 1081 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1